# proto-file: experimental/anomaly-detection/v1/generator/proto/query_template_config.proto
# proto-message: QueryTemplateConfigs
#
# Example individual (analytics class) -- "individual A", pinned via
# individual_edge_overrides (Requirement 4.3).
#
# One QueryTemplateConfigs holds exactly one markov_individual, so the two
# analytics individuals asked for by R4.3 are shipped as two sibling files:
# this one and individual_overrides_b.textproto. Both derive from the same
# analytics base graph but pin different edges to different weights, so the two
# derived individuals end up with differing edge weights.
#
# individual_edge_override is the highest-precedence per-edge mechanism: it pins
# an edge's weight outright (no variance/jitter, no random draw for that edge).
# Every (source_id, destination_id) below names a real edge of the base graph
# (aggregate_scan, time_window_report, order_rollup). Non-pinned edges keep
# their class weight; a single final renormalization makes each node's outgoing
# weights sum to 1.0.
#
# Individual A leans toward the windowed report: from the aggregate scan it is
# much more likely to move into the time-window report than the class baseline.

markov_individual {
  base_graph_path: "../../markov_analytics_user_config.textproto"

  # aggregate_scan: base weights are time_window_report=0.5, order_rollup=0.3,
  # aggregate_scan=0.2. Pin the edge into the windowed report higher so this
  # individual produces windowed reports more often than the class.
  individual_edge_overrides {
    source_id: "aggregate_scan"
    destination_id: "time_window_report"
    weight: 0.7
  }
}
